From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 01:18:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] drm/imagination: Support the Apple A10 (T8010) GPU

The A10's GPU is a Rogue core (CORE_ID 0x011b0602, legacy BVNC 13.10.6.2)
whose firmware is Apple's GFX firmware, not Imagination's: it runs on an
RTKit coprocessor behind the A7 IOP mailbox, powers the core through
Apple's "Fender" block and is initialized through a host-built memory
layout instead of IMG's FWIF.

Add an Apple firmware variant, selected by apple,t8010-gpu and
CONFIG_DRM_POWERVR_APPLE. It keeps powervr's device, GEM, VM and MMU
code and the UAPI, so the GPU appears as an ordinary powervr device, and
replaces what is specific to IMG's firmware:

- power: boot the coprocessor through apple-rtkit, then request a Fender
  power state, which is also the GPU's performance state, before any RGX
  register access;
- firmware init: build the init page, memory descriptors, firmware
  contexts and work rings (pvr_apple_layout.c) and wait for the firmware
  to accept them;
- jobs: compute, geometry and fragment jobs from the powervr UAPI,
  written to the firmware's rings and kicked through its doorbell
  endpoint, completed from the firmware's stamps and events, polled from
  an hrtimer (apple_poll_us). The firmware keeps one parameter-manager
  scene, so a geometry job waits for the previous fragment job's
  retirement and fragment-done event, and a fragment fence signals only
  after that event;
- clock: devfreq (simple_ondemand) on the GPU's OPP table, which the
  bootloader fills from the Apple device tree, the OPP level being the
  performance state; apple_perf_state fixes one;
- static device information, as the firmware carries no IMG header; no
  core clock and no RGX interrupt on the AP side.

Tested on the J71b (iPad 6): Hyprland through Zink and the Mesa G9P
Vulkan driver at 60 fps with live blur.

Assisted-by: LLM
